@prefix :    <https://arco.ai/ontology/core#> .
@prefix bfo: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_> .
@prefix ro:  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_> .
@prefix iao: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:<http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos:<http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix cco: <http://www.ontologyrepository.com/CommonCoreOntologies/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<https://arco.ai/ontology/core> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
  rdfs:label "ARCO Core (Gold Standard) — BFO/RO Aligned"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Core ontology with OWL restrictions for computable reasoning, with ICE-to-reality grounding via IAO is_about following BFO realist conventions. Uses ro:0000091 (has_disposition) per RO best practices." ;
  owl:versionInfo "Foundational ontology alignment: BFO 2020 (ISO/IEC 21838-2:2021) loaded as full upstream file from 03_TECHNICAL_CORE/ontology/imports/bfo-2020.owl. OBO Relations Ontology (RO) release 2025-12-17, IAO release 2026-03-30, and CCO release v1.7-2024-11-03 are loaded as ROBOT BOT-extracted slim modules per the OBO Foundry / ODK standard pattern, from imports/ro_bot.owl, imports/iao_bot.owl, and imports/cco_bot.owl respectively. Seed term lists are version-controlled at 03_TECHNICAL_CORE/ontology/imports/seeds/. See README 'Foundational ontology versions' for the full table." ;
  owl:imports <../ontology/imports/bfo-2020.owl> .
# NOTE: owl:imports uses a filesystem-relative path. rdflib does not follow
# owl:imports automatically; BFO is loaded explicitly by load_union_graph().
# Protégé and similar tools will resolve this correctly provided the repo
# directory structure is intact. If the file is moved, this path breaks silently.

# Design Decisions (rationale for the choices below):
#
#   System ⊑ ObjectAggregate: models the system as a structured aggregate
#   of material components. Defensible for component-level disposition
#   tracing. If a future system has stronger functional unity, revisit.
#
#   Capability ⊑ Disposition (not Function): ARCO's target is the
#   latent capability that the bearer's physical make-up grounds at design
#   time, regardless of whether the make-up was designed for that purpose.
#   BFO 2020 [064-001] defines Function as the disposition-subkind whose
#   bearer's physical make-up exists "either through evolution or through
#   intentional design, in order to realize processes of a certain sort";
#   typing as Function would narrow to designed-for cases and exclude
#   non-designed-in capacities that the hardware nonetheless grounds.
#   Disposition admits both, with Function instances participating as a
#   subkind. This is terminal, not a deferred upgrade. Provider intent
#   (EU AI Act Article 3(12)) lives at Gate 2 (cco:prescribes on the IUS),
#   ICE/representation-side, not at the BFO category of the hardware-side
#   disposition.
#
#   HighRiskSystem and AnnexIIITriggeringCapability live in the governance
#   extension, not here. Core contains only reality-side universals.
#   AnnexIII1aApplicableSystem: full three-gate (capability + intent +
#   scenario). Defined in governance extension.

#################################################################
# 0) REGULATORY-PROVENANCE ANNOTATION PROPERTY
#
# Declared in core (not governance) because annotation properties are
# inert under classification and benefit from being importable across
# files. Consumers of the governance extension inherit it automatically.
#
# :hasRegulatoryBasis is a narrow annotation-only exception to CLAUDE.md
# Invariant 6 (no custom properties). It is a subproperty of canonical
# CCO doctrinal_source and definition_source. Full CCO v1.7 declares
# doctrinal_source as a subproperty of definition_source; ARCO's BOT slim
# does not currently preserve that annotation-property hierarchy, so both
# parent links are stated here on the ARCO-local annotation property. This
# keeps the typed regulatory citations transparently visible to CCO consumers
# querying either doctrinal source or the broader definition-source predicate.
# The exception is documented at
# OPEN_PROBLEMS.md X.13 and docs/modeling_decisions/decisions_justification_map.md S3a.
#
# Constraints (do not relax without re-litigating S3a):
#   - Annotation-only; never used in classification, object-property
#     reasoning, or BFO relation modeling.
#   - Subproperty of cco:doctrinal_source preserves the sharper doctrinal
#     signal; subproperty of cco:definition_source preserves broader
#     canonical-consumer compatibility despite BOT dropping the upstream
#     annotation-property hierarchy.
#   - Applied to regulatory universals (the fiat-grouping classes) and
#     to OWL Axiom reifications on regulatory disjointness axioms.
#     NOT applied to leaf capability kinds — those are treated as bona
#     fide capability kinds (real participant-structure / cardinality
#     distinctions); the Smith-Varzi pattern is applied analogically
#     only at regulatory-extension partitions — see S3a.
#################################################################

:hasRegulatoryBasis a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
  rdfs:subPropertyOf cco:doctrinal_source , cco:definition_source ;
  rdfs:label "has regulatory basis"@en ;
  skos:definition "Annotation property pointing the annotated class or axiom at the regulatory text that determines its extension. Subproperty of cco:doctrinal_source, narrowed to legal-authority citations for regulatory fiat partitions and regulatory disjointness consequences. Also asserted as a subproperty of cco:definition_source because ARCO's BOT slim does not preserve the upstream CCO annotation-property hierarchy from cco:doctrinal_source to cco:definition_source; this keeps regulatory citations visible to consumers querying the broader CCO definition-source predicate while carrying the sharper doctrinal-source signal. Documented narrow exception to ARCO Invariant 6 (no custom properties); annotation-only, semantically inert under OWL-RL classification and HermiT DL reasoning. See docs/modeling_decisions/decisions_justification_map.md S3a and OPEN_PROBLEMS.md X.13."@en .

#################################################################
# 1) REALITY-SIDE UNIVERSALS (BFO)
#
# Design Decision: We use ro:0000091 (has_disposition) rather than
# ro:0000053 (bearer_of) because:
#   - has_disposition is more specific in the RO property hierarchy
#   - has_disposition has domain: independent continuant, range: disposition
#   - bearer_of is generic (covers qualities, roles, functions, dispositions)
#   - This aligns with OBO Foundry best practices
# See section 5 for the parallel ro:0000052 → bfo:0000197 subPropertyOf
# binding (BFO range adds the IC constraint RO removed from characteristic_of).
#################################################################

:System rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "AI System"@en ;
  skos:definition "An object aggregate of one or more material components, used in ARCO as the assessment target for EU AI Act Annex III gates. The class itself does not formally restrict to AI; whether the described artifact is an AI system within the meaning of Article 3(1) is a human-adjudicated input commitment held by the instance author — ARCO does not evaluate that threshold, and disposition kinds do not establish it (see LIMITATIONS)."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000027 ; # Object Aggregate
  rdfs:subClassOf [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty bfo:0000051 ; # has part
    owl:someValuesFrom :SystemComponent
  ] .

:SystemComponent rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "System Component"@en ;
  skos:definition "A material part of an AI system. Software is not a system component; it is information content that runs on hardware."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Material component of an AI system (bfo:Object). Software is NOT a SystemComponent — it is an ICE (see SoftwareArtifact). has_part between an ObjectAggregate and an ICE would be a BFO category error." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000030 . # Object

:HardwareComponent rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Hardware Component"@en ;
  skos:definition "A physical part of an AI system, such as a sensor module, processor, or integrated unit, that bears the system's capabilities."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf :SystemComponent ;
  rdfs:subClassOf [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty ro:0000091 ; # has disposition (RO-specific)
    owl:someValuesFrom :Capability
  ] .

:Capability rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "AI Capability"@en ;
  skos:definition "A disposition borne by a system component whose realization is the component participating in a process that produces the output the disposition is directed toward. ARCO subclasses this to enumerate capability kinds named by Annex III (biometric identification, creditworthiness evaluation, and others)."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000016 . # Disposition

:BiometricIdentificationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Biometric Identification Capability"@en ;
  skos:definition "The capability to identify an unknown person by comparing their biometric data against many stored records (one-to-many matching)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Gate 1 capability for ARCO's Annex III 1(a) encoding; full applicability also needs the remote-identification intended use and a natural-person use scenario. The operative trigger is Annex III item 1(a) under Article 6(2); Article 3(35) only defines 1:N identification (as distinct from 1:1 verification, Article 3(36)), and the 'remote' differentia (Article 3(41)) sits on the Gate-2 process, not on this capability."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Identification (finding an unknown person, one-to-many) is a different kind of thing from verification (confirming a claimed identity, one-to-one), not the same disposition. A real disposition of the hardware, present whether realized or not; ARCO models it as disjoint from verification and creditworthiness evaluation, and as a member of the :AnnexIIITriggeringCapability set it is what Gate 1 of Annex III 1(a) looks for and what fires the latent :HighRiskSystem flag from Gate 1 alone. The 1:N content is prose and regulation only, not an axiom; the reasoner sees only the disjointness, the union membership, and the gate's has_disposition filler. Remoteness is not part of this capability, so the name should not be read as implying remote operation." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf :Capability .

:BiometricVerificationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Biometric Verification Capability"@en ;
  skos:definition "The capability to confirm a claimed identity by comparing biometric data against a single reference (one-to-one matching)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Out of scope of Annex III 1(a) by the operative carve-out for biometric verification (Annex III 1(a) second sentence; Recitals 15, 17). Article 3(36) defines the 1:1 verification this names, as distinct from 1:N identification (Article 3(35))."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Verification (confirming a claimed identity, one-to-one) is a different kind of thing from identification (finding an unknown person, one-to-many), not a sub-type of it. ARCO models them as disjoint capabilities and deliberately leaves verification out of the :AnnexIIITriggeringCapability set, so a verification-only system fires no latent high-risk flag; and because Gate 1 of Annex III 1(a) keys on identification, the disjointness blocks 1(a) applicability even when the other gates hold. The 1:1 distinction itself lives in the definition and the regulation, not in an axiom; the reasoner enforces only the disjointness and the union exclusion. A real (reality-side) disposition of the hardware; do not confuse with identification." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf :Capability .

:CreditworthinessEvaluationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Creditworthiness Evaluation Capability"@en ;
  skos:definition "The capability to evaluate a person's creditworthiness or establish their credit score."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Gate 1 capability for ARCO's Annex III 5(b) encoding; full applicability also needs the creditworthiness-evaluation intended use and a natural-person use scenario. The operative trigger is Annex III item 5(b) under Article 6(2); the Act gives no Article 3 definition of creditworthiness, so 5(b)'s own text is the only anchor (do not pin to a definitional article)."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Evaluating a person's credit standing, a real, reality-side capability the hardware bears. Do not confuse it with benefits-eligibility scoring for public assistance, which is Annex III 5(a) territory (unmodeled), not this class. ARCO models it as disjoint from the two biometric capabilities, and as a member of the :AnnexIIITriggeringCapability set it is the Gate 1 capability for Annex III 5(b) and fires the latent :HighRiskSystem flag. Gate 1 is ARCO's design-time evidential addition, stricter than the Annex III text (LIMITATIONS §3.9)." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf :Capability .

# Pairwise disjointness: these are genuinely different capability kinds.
# A component cannot simultaneously bear biometric identification AND
# creditworthiness evaluation as the SAME disposition. Prevents mis-typing.
:BiometricIdentificationCapability owl:disjointWith :BiometricVerificationCapability .
:BiometricIdentificationCapability owl:disjointWith :CreditworthinessEvaluationCapability .
:BiometricVerificationCapability owl:disjointWith :CreditworthinessEvaluationCapability .

# Regulatory-fiat annotation on the identification-vs-verification disjointness.
# The underlying 1:N identification vs 1:1 verification cardinality is a real
# capability-kind distinction (different participant structures, different
# realizable process kinds). The regulatory CONSEQUENCE — that verification is
# carved out of Annex III 1(a) — is a fiat cut grounded in Regulation (EU)
# 2024/1689. See OPEN_PROBLEMS.md X.13 and decisions_justification_map.md S3a.
[ a owl:Axiom ;
  owl:annotatedSource :BiometricIdentificationCapability ;
  owl:annotatedProperty owl:disjointWith ;
  owl:annotatedTarget :BiometricVerificationCapability ;
  :hasRegulatoryBasis "Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Article 3(35) (biometric identification definition), Article 3(36) (biometric verification definition, 1:1), Article 3(41) (remote biometric identification system definition), Annex III item 1(a), Recitals 15 and 17"@en ;
  :hasRegulatoryBasis "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj"^^xsd:anyURI ;
  skos:scopeNote "The 1:N identification and 1:1 verification process kinds have real technical content: different required participants, different realizable process kinds, different evaluability metrics (FAR/FRR for verification; rank-N accuracy for identification). The REGULATORY CONSEQUENCE of this disjointness — that biometric verification is excluded from Annex III 1(a) per Recital 15 / Recital 17 / Annex III 1(a) operative carve-out — is a fiat legal cut grounded in Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 Articles 3(35), 3(36), and 3(41). Smith-Varzi fiat/bona-fide distinction used analogically per S3a: the capability kinds are bona fide; the regulatory exclusion that rides on this disjointness is fiat."@en
] .

:OperationalProcess rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Operational Process"@en ;
  skos:definition "A process in which an AI system realizes one or more of its capabilities at deployment."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000015 ; # Process
  rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ro:0000057 ; owl:someValuesFrom :System ] ,                 # has participant
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty bfo:0000055 ; owl:someValuesFrom :Capability ] . # realizes

#################################################################
# 2) REPRESENTATION-SIDE UNIVERSALS (IAO)
#################################################################

:SoftwareArtifact rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Software Artifact"@en ;
  skos:definition "Software content (model weights, code, configuration) used by an AI system. Modeled as information content rather than a physical part, so dispositions inhere in the hardware that runs the software."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Software as a generically dependent continuant (ICE). Dispositions inhere in the material bearer that concretizes the software, not in the software artifact itself. Software relates to systems via concretization in material components, not via parthood. Do not type software as SystemComponent." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 . # Information Content Entity

:InformationOutput rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Information Output"@en ;
  skos:definition "An information artifact produced as the output of a process, such as an assessment report or a determination record."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 . # Information Content Entity

:RegulatoryContent rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Regulatory Content"@en ;
  skos:definition "An information artifact representing a passage of regulatory text, such as an Annex III item or a specific Article condition."@en ;
  rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 . # Information Content Entity

:ComplianceDetermination rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Compliance Determination Result"@en ;
  skos:definition "A Descriptive Information Content Entity (ICE) that records the outcome of an ARCO entailment about a specific AI system — for example, an applicability entailment under a particular Annex III category, a latent-risk capability-precondition flag, or a non-applicability finding. This class is the parent ICE class for ARCO-side records of entailment outcomes; it is distinguished from a legal determination by a regulator or conformity assessment body under EU AI Act Articles 16-29, which the ontology does not produce."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "ICE-vs-reality cut: a ComplianceDetermination is a representation-side record of what ARCO entailed about a system, not the entailment itself and not a change in the system's ontological kind. It is also NOT a legal determination by a regulator or conformity assessment body under EU AI Act Articles 16-29 — those are produced by competent authorities under the Act's procedural framework, not by ARCO. Subclasses (e.g., :HighRiskDetermination) refine the kind of entailment recorded." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 . # Information Content Entity

:HighRiskDetermination rdf:type owl:Class ;
  rdfs:label "High-Risk Determination"@en ;
  skos:definition "A Compliance Determination ICE that records either (a) the latent-risk capability-precondition flag entailment (membership in :HighRiskSystem, fired by Gate 1 alone — an Annex III triggering capability is borne by a system component), or (b) a category-specific Annex III applicability entailment (e.g., membership in :AnnexIII1aApplicableSystem or :AnnexIII5bApplicableSystem, fired by all three gates: capability, prescribed process, and affected role). The IRI is retained for backward compatibility. This class is NOT equivalent to a legal high-risk determination by a regulator or conformity assessment body under EU AI Act Articles 16-29."@en ;
  rdfs:comment "The IRI is retained for backward compatibility. This class is a Descriptive ICE recording an ARCO entailment (latent-risk flag from Gate 1, or three-gate Annex III applicability) and is NOT equivalent to a legal high-risk determination by a regulator or conformity assessment body under EU AI Act Articles 16-29. The bifurcation between (a) the Gate-1-only latent-risk flag (HighRiskSystem) and (b) the three-gate category-specific applicability entailments (AnnexIII1aApplicableSystem, AnnexIII5bApplicableSystem) is recorded by the certificate's PRIMARY vs. LATENT-RISK FLAG split; this class is the ICE container for either kind of record." ;
  rdfs:subClassOf :ComplianceDetermination .

#################################################################
# 3) BRIDGE AXIOMS — moved to governance extension
#
# AnnexIIITriggeringCapability and HighRiskSystem are defined in
# ARCO_governance_extension.ttl. They reference regulatory grouping
# concepts and belong in the regulatory domain layer, not core.
#################################################################

#################################################################
# 4) RELATION ANCHORS (labels for UI/debugging)
#################################################################

# BFO classes
bfo:0000015 rdfs:label "Process" .
bfo:0000016 rdfs:label "Disposition" .
bfo:0000023 rdfs:label "Role" .
bfo:0000027 rdfs:label "Object Aggregate" .
bfo:0000030 rdfs:label "Object" .
bfo:0000040 rdfs:label "Material Entity" .

# BFO relations
bfo:0000050 rdfs:label "part of" .
bfo:0000051 rdfs:label "has part" .
bfo:0000055 rdfs:label "realizes" .

# RO relations
ro:0000052 rdfs:label "characteristic of" .
ro:0000053 rdfs:label "bearer of" .
ro:0000057 rdfs:label "has participant" .
ro:0000087 rdfs:label "has role" .
ro:0000091 rdfs:label "has disposition" .

# BFO 2020 inherence direct label (referenced by the subPropertyOf binding below)
bfo:0000197 rdfs:label "inheres in" .

#################################################################
# 5) EXTERNAL PROPERTY COMMITMENT — subPropertyOf binding to BFO 2020
#################################################################

ro:0000052 rdfs:subPropertyOf bfo:0000197 ;  # inheres in
  rdfs:comment "ARCO commits RO 'characteristic of' (ro:0000052) as a specialization of bfo:0000197 (BFO 2020 'inheres in') so the reasoner inherits BFO's IndependentContinuant range on the inferred inherence triple. RO removed the range to support qualities of processes or ICE; ARCO does not model those cases. Bounded enforcement: catches wrong-typed bearer only when typed as a disjoint sibling of IC. Same axiomatic shape as cco:designates rdfs:subPropertyOf iao:0000136 at ARCO_governance_extension.ttl:139 (note: that binding widens cco:designates up into iao:is_about; this binding narrows ro:0000052 down through BFO's stricter range). See LIMITATIONS.md §3.8."@en .

# IAO relations
iao:0000136 rdfs:label "is about" .
